---
import '../styles/global.css';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head
    ><meta charset="utf-8" /><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" /><title>
      Field Relay case study
    </title></head
  ><body
    ><main>
      <p class="eyebrow">FIELD RELAY / CASE STUDY</p><h1>
        Making messy order exports reviewable.
      </h1><p class="lede">
        A fictional operations desk receives partner CSVs that use different headers and sometimes
        repeat, conflict, or corrupt an order. The question is whether a person can see what will
        happen before a destination is touched.
      </p><h2>Decision design</h2><p>
        An adapter maps each partner’s headers. Validation keeps the physical source row and raw
        cells. A partner plus order ID is the business key: exact duplicates are suppressed, while
        every conflicting or invalid variant is held. Region rules select a destination; Central and
        high-value orders wait for a local approval.
      </p><h2>Replay experiment</h2><p>
        Choose Northstar, validate, inject an outage, then deliver. Turn the outage off and retry. A
        stable key and fingerprint produce one simulated delivery; replay produces a visible skip.
        Change a delivered payload and it is held rather than inherited as a retry.
      </p><h2>Failure found in testing</h2><p>
        Early versions treated a capped event list as the delivery registry. After enough events, an
        old successful order could be forgotten. The workbench now keeps a memory-only session
        registry separate from its 500-event display.
      </p><h2>Operational boundary</h2><p>
        This demo has no vendors, accounts, storage, network uploads, authentication, or real
        delivery. It is a local, inspectable simulation.
      </p><p>
        <a href={base}>Open the simulation</a> · <a
          href="https://github.com/mastodonsharkgirl/field-relay">Inspect the source</a
        >
      </p>
    </main></body
  >
</html>
